Subject: [DSA prefill] skip index blocks that are entirely -1 padding

Base:      sglang git 29481685462732237d80d86076d6563e1f658102
           (python/sglang/kernels/ops/attention/dsa/tilelang_kernel.py is clean at
           that commit in this image; pristine copy in patches/base/)
File:      python/sglang/kernels/ops/attention/dsa/tilelang_kernel.py
Related:   patches/rejected/04-dsa-prefill-first-slot-guard.patch is a REJECTED variant that
           stacks on top of this one, replacing this patch's any-valid reduce with a single
           first-slot test. It is faster (1.067x vs 1.039x isolated on the prefill shape,
           roughly +0.64% more end to end) and was not adopted because it depends on an
           invariant four different topk backends would each have to preserve. Read its
           header before "improving" the guard here -- the cheaper predicate was measured,
           not overlooked.

Measurement (ISL 8192 / OSL 1024 / concurrency 64 / 192 prompts / TP8, frozen config):

    output_throughput   1487.89 tok/s  vs baseline 1468.358 tok/s   = +1.33%
      arms: results/a_only_r1_20260820_131134 1487.08
            results/a_only_r2_20260820_131413 1488.70
      baseline: 7 runs over 3 server sessions, mean 1468.358, peak-to-peak 0.306%
    Noise floor for this workload is 0.39% peak-to-peak across restarts (measured
    over 21 runs and 9 server sessions; see FINDINGS.md), so this is ~3.4x the floor.

    Isolated (analysis/bench_dsa_prefill.py, 16384x8, topk 2048, interleaved):
      live cat+cast+tilelang   4.415 ms
      + block-skip             4.248 ms   = 1.039x

    Accuracy: gated as part of A+B, gsm8k 5-shot strict-match 0.973465 +/- 0.004427
    vs baseline 0.970432 +/- 0.004666 (eval_results/ab_gate_20260820_125928).

What it does
    sparse_mla_fwd_decode_partial_fp8 walks all topk/block_I index blocks for every
    query and *masks* the -1 padding: it still gathers block_I x 576 B of KV and still
    issues all five GEMMs, then discards the result via a -inf score. At this workload
    12.1% of all blocks processed are entirely padding. This adds a block-uniform
    any-valid reduce over the mask the kernel already builds and branches the gather
    and the GEMMs around it.

    Eliding an all-padding block is bit-exact rather than an approximation: every score
    in it is -inf, so exp2 gives 0 -- it contributes nothing to sumexp and nothing to
    acc_o -- and m_i is reduced with clear=False so a block of -inf cannot lower it.

    The guard is compiled in only for prefill-sized calls (>= _SKIP_EMPTY_MIN_TOKENS
    tokens). At decode no block is ever all-padding, the guard never fires, and it
    costs a measured 8% (34.55 -> 37.32 us). skip_empty defaults to False and, left
    False, the TVM Script parser drops the branch, the reduce and the valid_i32 write
    entirely: the factory then emits a byte-identical kernel to the pre-patch one
    (verified by string comparison of get_kernel_source() at both splits), and decode
    output is bitwise identical.

    No server flag, environment variable or workload parameter is changed.

